.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: rehex(1) man page. Per-line encoding decision, the -S and -U conversion map, all 5 options.
.\"
.TH REHEX 1 "2026-08-29" "rehex 1.7" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
rehex \- apply or remove $HEX[] encoding on line-oriented input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rehex
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR spec ]
.RB [ \-U
.IR spec ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rehex
copies its input to standard output, wrapping any line that contains a byte
which does not survive plain-text handling in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]\fR.
Embedded NULs, control characters and high-bit bytes are the usual
triggers.  With no
.I file
argument it reads standard input; the literal name
.B stdin
may be used among the file arguments.
.PP
The decision is per line, not per byte: if any byte in a line is marked in
the conversion map, the whole line is hex-encoded.  The wrapper holds the
line's bytes in lowercase hexadecimal, so the password
.B cafe
with an acute accent on the final e \(em five characters, six bytes in
UTF-8 \(em becomes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$HEX[636166c3a9]
.fi
.RE
.PP
That form is pure ASCII, contains no delimiter or control character, and is
understood on input by
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hashpipe (1),
.BR getpass (1),
.BR dedupe (1)
and
.B rehex
itself, which is what makes it the toolchain's interchange format for
awkward text.
.PP
.B \-u
reverses the transformation, decoding
.B $HEX[]
wrappers back to raw bytes.  That produces output which is no longer safe
to pass through line-oriented tools, which is the entire reason the
encoding exists.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
Decode
.B $HEX[]
instead of encoding.
.TP
.BI \-S " spec"
Mark characters as requiring encoding.
.I spec
is a comma-separated list of literal characters, decimal values,
.BI 0x NN
hexadecimal values, and ranges such as
.BR a\-f ,
.B 0\-32
or
.BR 0x1a\-0x1f .
The resulting 256-entry map is printed to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-U " spec"
As
.BR \-S ,
but unmarks.
.B "\-U 0\-255"
disables encoding entirely, making
.B rehex
a pass-through.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage error.  Unreadable input files are
reported and skipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make an arbitrary binary-contaminated wordlist safe to pipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rehex messy.txt > clean.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The round trip is exact.  Encoding and then decoding returns the original
bytes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf 'caf\\303\\251\\n' | rehex
$HEX[636166c3a9]

$ printf 'caf\\303\\251\\n' | rehex | rehex \-u | od \-An \-tx1
   63  61  66  c3  a9  0a
.fi
.RE
.PP
Treat the colon as needing encoding too, so that
.BR mdxfind (1)
result parsing cannot be confused by a password containing one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf 'pass:word\\n' | rehex \-S ':'
$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Wrap the raw digest an
.BR hx (1)
.B _bin
expression writes, which is otherwise unsafe to pipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hx \-p alpha 'md5_bin(pass)' | rehex
$HEX[2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9]
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dedupe (1),
.BR splitlen (1),
.BR getpass (1),
.BR rling (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
